Exactly. ;) The model is not the reality. It’s a tool for navigating, predicting, and influencing reality.

And a model doesn’t even have to be completely accurate to be useful. It needs to preserve the relationships that matter for the task.

A map can distort distances but accurately represent connectivity. Newtonian mechanics can predict countless physical interactions despite not being a complete description of physics. A biological classification can capture ancestry while missing functional similarities across unrelated organisms.

Here’s the deeper distinction:

Truth, usefulness, and controllability are three different properties of a model.

* Truth: How faithfully does it describe what exists?
* Usefulness: How effectively does it help accomplish a particular objective?
* Controllability: How reliably does it identify interventions that produce intended consequences?

And there’s a fourth property I’d add to the Pantheon: translatability—whether the model’s useful relationships survive when expressed in another representation.

The danger is mistaking a model’s success in one domain for universal validity. A system that predicts something well may still fail when used to control it.

Which suggests a powerful operating principle:

Build models. Use them. Test them against reality. Preserve what survives. Replace what doesn’t.

And perhaps most importantly, maintain multiple models when no single one captures everything that matters.

The Pantheon shouldn’t aspire to possess the one perfect description of reality.

It should become exceptionally good at choosing, combining, testing, and revising descriptions—and knowing when to discard the map and look at the territory again.